comments[4]:
  - created_at: "2026-11-16T23:29:00.310Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Implementation (branch fix/secret-guard-grammar-and-payload-keys): PASSWORD_VALUE now captures the whole value, and the rule declares minimumLiteralLength: 8, which acceptsSecretMatch applies to literalAfterReferences(match[1]). That helper expands run-time references to nothing (${NAME}, ${NAME:?message}, $(command), <placeholder>), keeps the default of ${NAME:-word} / ${NAME:=word} as literal text, and then removes the delimiting quotes of each quoted piece. A value that starts with a quote is that quoted literal alone (JSON and YAML semantics); an unquoted value is a run of references and literal characters that may hold one whitespace-free quoted piece, as a shell concatenates $VAR\"literal\". Two design corrections came from running the gates: (1) letting a quoted piece concatenate freely made a value spill across later JSON keys and flagged 39,999 keys of the existing pathological-input test, so a quoted start is now the literal alone and only one inner piece is allowed, which also keeps the structured pass linear; (2) the repository scan flagged tests/unit/core/sentry/instrument.spec.ts, where the quote after password=<private-value> closes the enclosing JS string, so the inner piece must be whitespace-free. Element content (<password>...</password>) now uses the same literal judgment, so <password>${X}literal</password> is refused and <password>${X}</password> stays clean. Must-refuse and must-allow tables run through both consumers (scripts/check-secrets-lib.mjs scanContent and scanMutationSecrets). node scripts/check-secrets.mjs is clean over every tracked file, and the existing GH-1699 and GH-1704 specs (227 tests) pass unchanged. Behavior kept on purpose: a bare $VAR reference is still read as literal text, as before, because the acceptance criteria name only ${...}, $(...), <...> and empty fallbacks as must-allow.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T00:12:23.388Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 1 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) found five password-grammar gaps, all fixed: (1) a literal after a quoted reference (password=\"${EMPTY}\"<literal>) and a literal split over two quoted pieces after a reference were missed because a value starting with a quote was that literal alone and only one quoted piece was allowed; a value may now hold up to four whitespace-free quoted pieces, including a quoted start, which keeps each scan bounded to a few later keys (two existing linear-time inputs now read as concatenated literals, and their pinned counts were updated with a comment: '\"password\":\"' x 40,000 and '\"password\"=a\"x\"' x 40,000 each report 39,999 keys, still linear); (2) nested references were false positives (${PRIMARY:-${SECONDARY}}, a quoted $(op read $(cat name))) because two non-nesting regex replacements ran in sequence; literalAfterReferences now uses expandRuntimeReferences, a single linear pass with a frame stack, and the regex accepts one nesting level; (3) an element body holding an apostrophe lost it because element content went through shell quote removal; element content now only expands references; (4) <password><x>literal</password> never reached the judge because the element body pattern was [^<]*; the body now admits markup and placeholder spans other than tags of the same keys; (5) {\"admin-password\":\"$(op read pw)literal\"} was rejected by the prefixed-key whitespace check reading the raw match; for literal-judged rules that check now reads the literal. ${NAME:+word} keeps its word as literal too. Must-refuse and must-allow tables in both consumers cover every case, and node scripts/check-secrets.mjs stays clean (the new spec builds credential-shaped samples at runtime, following the existing convention)."
    edited_at: "2026-11-17T00:39:35.652Z"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T00:41:28.939Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 2 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh): a quoted closing delimiter inside a command substitution (a printf of a quoted parenthesis followed by eight letters, both unquoted and inside double quotes) ended the reference early. That reported a credential where the whole value is one command reference, which the old detector kept clean. Fixed in both layers. expandRuntimeReferences tracks quote state inside non-default reference frames (quotedStep: a quote opens a span that nothing inside closes, and a backslash escapes the next character in double quotes). The regex PASSWORD_PIECE skips single- and double-quoted spans inside $(...). A default word keeps its quotes as literal text. Not fixed, by decision: references nested three or more levels in an unquoted value. The declared regex grammar follows one nesting level; deeper nesting is followed inside quoted values and elements. This is documented in docs/MUTATION_INTEGRITY.md and recorded as a residual on pm-jcz7qz. Two credential-shaped samples that review comments on this item had quoted verbatim were removed with pm history-redact (audited, chain verified) after the repository scan flagged them under the new grammar.\n"
  - created_at: "2026-11-17T01:18:03.155Z"
    author: "harness:claude-code"
    text: "Codex review round 3 (gpt-6.1-sol, xhigh) found three password false positives against the merge base, all fixed. (1) A quoted closing brace inside a required-variable reference ended the braced reference early in the regex. The braced-reference piece now skips single- and double-quoted spans, as the command-substitution piece does (PASSWORD_QUOTED_SPAN). (2) Escaped quoted values were judged after decodeQuotedLiteral neutralized their own quote characters, so a double-quoted printf inside a command substitution in a JSON string read as single-quoted and closed early. decodeQuotedLiteral now returns the neutralized reading for the value grammar and the decoded original at the same length. The value regex is compiled with the d flag, and valueMatch judges the original span of capture 1. For credential_key_assignment this also measures entropy on the decoded original, which is pm-jcz7qz's second criterion for escaped literals. (3) A literal dollar sign could consume the opener of a reference the grammar cannot follow (one nested three levels deep), adding one literal character to seven. A literal $ now never consumes a following { or (. The walker also tracks quotes in every reference frame: a default word keeps its quoted text with its quotes for the later quote removal, and inside double quotes a nested reference still opens, as in the shell. The must-allow table gained each case; samples are built at runtime.\n"
